WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
Fine-tuning.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.067
Knowing when not to fine-tune is worth more than knowing the training code.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:12.333
Fine-tuning means continuing to train an already pretrained model on your own data.

4
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:14.833
The goal is to specialize how it behaves.

5
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:17.667
But it's a tool you reach for third, not first.

6
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:20.148
Here's how to decide.

7
00:00:20.148 --> 00:00:22.100
Start by asking what is missing.

8
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.228
If you simply haven't asked well, prompt first.

9
00:00:25.228 --> 00:00:27.333
It's cheap, instant and reversible.

10
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.456
If the model lacks fresh, citable facts, that's a knowledge gap.

11
00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:31.933
Use RAG.

12
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:37.033
If it lacks a house style, a strict format, or a reflex, that's a behaviour gap.

13
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:37.633
Fine-tune.

14
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:42.112
The classic mistake is fine-tuning to inject facts.

15
00:00:42.112 --> 00:00:47.833
Facts baked into weights go stale, can't be cited, and can't be removed for one customer.

16
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.876
Instead, keep knowledge in the context window.

17
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:52.367
Behaviour is what sticks.

18
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:55.933
Write a memo like this for every project.

19
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:59.333
Fresh facts arrive daily, so that's a job for RAG.

20
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:03.433
A strict report template is a style problem, so that's fine-tuning.

21
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:07.600
And the base model already summarizes well, so no capability gap.

22
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.533
For Mission Control RAG you'd likely use both.

23
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.100
Retrieval brings in the report content.

24
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.100
A small fine-tune, maybe later, makes answers come out in flight ops format.

25
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.425
That saves about four hundred prompt tokens on format instructions.

26
00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:25.167
Be ready to defend that memo.

27
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:27.167
Let's recap.

28
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:29.433
Fine-tuning specializes behaviour.

29
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:32.400
Go in order: prompt, then RAG, then fine-tune.

30
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.300
RAG fills knowledge gaps.

31
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.981
And never inject facts into weights.

32
00:01:36.981 --> 00:01:38.833
Next up: instruction tuning.
